I have a PexMethod PUT_AddContract that I want to write a contract test for using Microsoft's Pex. Here is the method:
public int Add(T item)
{
    if(_fixedSize && (_size >= _array.Length))
    {
        return -1;
    }

    if(item == null)
    {
        throw new ArgumentNullException("item");
    }

    if(_size == _array.Length)
    {
        Grow(item);
    }
    else
    {
        _array[_size++] = item;
    }

    return _size - 1;
}

And according to the method's contract, when the method Add is called, the item it tries to add to the ArrayList is checked to see whether it is null, afterwards, it either adds the item to the ArrayList at the end of the ArrayList, if there is space (the size of the ArrayList isn't its maximum length), or it resizes the ArrayList to accommodate the new item if there isn't. If the ArrayList is fixedSize (i.e., defined at initialization as such), it will not be resized, and it will instead return -1 and not add the item.
The Pex observers are used for displaying the values that I'm observing and any that might be important in the test.
My question is: How do I properly set up the contract test to ensure that the method Add correctly fulfils its contracts? How would you observe and assert certain conditions?

A: Let's proceed step-by-step:
1. AssumeUnderTest: Set up the test object that you're assuming is under test.
2. State Under Test: State the object or objects you're testing.
3. Invocation Under Test: Define the parameters against which you should invoke the method under test.
4. Conditions Under Test: Set your conditions for the expected results.
5. Method Under Test: Call the method under test with the determined parameters.
6. Observed results: Record the results of the method call.
7. Check results of the method under test.

Here is an example of how it could look like:
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[PexMethod]
public void AddTest([PexAssumeUnderTest]CustomArrayList customList, [PexArgValue(null)] string item)
{
   var oldSize = customList.Count;

   var result = customList.Add(item);

   Assert.IsTrue(customList.Count == oldSize + 1 || (customList.Count == -1 && oldSize != customList.MaxSize));
   Assert.IsTrue(customList.Contains(item));
}

Let's breakdown the code above:
- [PexAssumeUnderTest] CustomArrayList customList: We're assuming an object under test - customList of type CustomArrayList
- [PexArgValue(null)] string item: We're providing a value to the method Add. In this case, we're trying to test what happens when we add a null value to the customList
- We're calling the Add method and storing the result in the variable 'result'
- We're checking two conditions:
     - If the size of the customList increased by 1, then we successfully added the item to the list.
     - If Count is -1, then the customList is at its fixed size, so we can't add the item
        (but in this case we added an item to the customList with the max size)
- We're asserting that the item is in the customList by calling the Contains method

So this test is verifying that the Add method increases the 'Count' property of the ArrayList by 1 (at least), and that the item has been added successfully.
The conditions and the observed results need to be carefully verified with the requirements. Depending on the specific requirements, other assertions might also be needed--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX && New_arrListCount >= Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
