The ArrayList Contract Test is supposed to test the Add function of the ArrayList class. This function accepts an integer input and returns an integer output, which should correspond to the index at which the item was successfully added to the ArrayList. The test is supposed to ensure that the following:

1. An item is not already in the ArrayList.
2. The 'Count' property of the ArrayList increases by 1 when an item is added.
3. The index of the newly added item is same as the 'Count' of the list.
4. The 'IndexOf' and 'LastIndexOf' of the newly added item return the correct index.
5. The 'Contains' method returns 'true' for the newly added item.

I use the NUnit framework and Moles for my unit tests and Pex for generating test cases.
What I am looking to achieve is the ability to observe multiple values during the test execution so that I can understand their states during different steps of the test without having to manually check the states of multiple variables at different stages.

Can someone please point out which part I should change so as to enable me to see more data during the test execution while using the Pex class?

A: At the moment, Pex (and therefore, PexClass) does not support observing multiple values during the test execution. If you need to observe multiple states of certain variables as the test is running, you would need to manually check those variables in each step of the test, or possibly refactor the contract test to be more specific.

I tried to avoid the [PexMethod] attribute to automatically see all the variable states but it seems there is no way. In order for Pex to 'observe' your variables, you have to manually check them as mentioned earlier. This seems to be a common issue with Pex and automated testing in general where developers often need to manually intervene to refactor or enhance test cases.

Adding this to my list of TODO tasks. I'll be working on this as soon as possible.

Source: In the discussion thread of the bug report (https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/you-are-encountering-problems-with-a-particular-tool-or-feature-in-microsoft-visual-studio-751c5e49-6bb5-255c-9503-7c47713606a3)

This feature is not currently in NUnit but it is available in MSTest, where it is explicitly stated that you need to manually check variables. And Pex is just a wrapper for MSTest.
It's tough to know why this feature hasn't been implemented yet, and I understand there is a lot to be desired from automated testing tools.
But I understand your point and this feature suggestion should encourage them developers to improve the tool. 

Unfortunately, this is probably the best solution if you want to use Pex, or another automated testing tool. I hope Microsoft will integrate this feature into the next version of tools.

To summarize, it seems to be a limitation at present.

A: For observing multiple values with Pex and MSTest, you could use the PexObserve methods as you have done in your code snippets. However, the state of the ArrayList alone in a Pex method will not give you much, instead you are observing individual elements of the ArrayList which might not be a good option.

To get meaningful results, separate the conditions you want to test into multiple methods. For example, one method could check if the count increases by one, another could check whether the last index of the item is the same as the count (or even better is equals to an expected value). Similar methods could be used to check the remaining conditions.

You can also check what you are testing in the coverage report. For example, you can add elements to an ArrayList and then check the count of non-existent keys. The coverage report will tell you what tests you need to write.

On another note, Pex needs a method to test. Pex is not designed to test a class without a test method. You can have many methods within the same class decorated with PexMethod or PexClass, but they all have to be connected somehow to a test method. If all your methods are connected to a single method decorated with PexClass, Pex will find only a single path.

I would recommend the Pex documentation and this thread for more information--------------------	    	PexAssert.IsTrue( New_arrListIndexOfX>=Old_arrListCount );

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that new count after adding x is greater than old count.
	    	PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount>Old_arrListCount);
	    
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" x appears at last after adding x.
	    	PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListCount - 1);
	    
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" x appears in the array after adding x.
	    	PexAssert.IsTrue (New_arrListContainsX== true);
            
        }
    }
